public class BubbleSort {
    public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                    // 交换arr[j]和arr[j+1]
                    int temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
        bubbleSort(arr);
        System.out.println("排序后的数组:");
        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}
```
- **Correctness**:
    - The code correctly implements the bubble sort algorithm. It compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order, gradually "bubbling" the larger elements to the end of the array. The `main` method demonstrates the sorting functionality by creating an array, sorting it, and printing the sorted array.
- **Readability**:
    - The code is fairly readable. The naming of variables (`n` for the array length, `temp` for the temporary variable during the swap) is intuitive. However, adding Javadoc comments to the `bubbleSort` method to explain its purpose, parameters, and return - value (even though it's a `void` method, it's good to document what it does) would improve readability further. Also, adding some whitespace or comments to separate the inner and outer loops conceptually could make it even clearer.
- **Efficiency**:
    - The code has a time complexity of $O(n^2)$ in the worst and average cases, which is the expected time complexity for the bubble sort algorithm. There are no obvious in - code performance bottlenecks, but bubble sort is not the most efficient sorting algorithm for large datasets compared to more advanced algorithms like quicksort or mergesort.
- **Edge Cases**:
    - The code does not handle edge cases well. For example, it does not check if the input array is `null` or empty. If a `null` array is passed to the `bubbleSort` method, it will throw a `NullPointerException`. Also, if an empty array is passed, the current implementation will still execute the loops, but it could be optimized to avoid unnecessary loop iterations.
- **Best Practices**:
    - It follows the best practice of having a `main` method as the entry - point for testing the functionality. However, it could be improved by following more comprehensive Java best practices such as adding proper error handling for edge cases, using Javadoc comments for better documentation, and potentially separating the sorting logic from the testing logic in a more modular way (e.g., by having a separate test class if this were part of a larger project). 
```java
/**
 * This class contains the implementation of the bubble sort algorithm.
 * Bubble sort is a simple sorting algorithm that repeatedly steps through the list,
 * compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order.
 * The pass through the list is repeated until the list is sorted.
 */
public class BubbleSort {
    /**
     * Sorts the given integer array using the bubble sort algorithm.
     *
     * @param arr The array of integers to be sorted. The method will sort this array in - place.
     *            If the array is null or empty, the method will not perform any sorting and return without throwing an exception.
     */
    public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length == 0) {
            return;
        }
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            // Outer loop for the number of passes
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                // Inner loop for comparing adjacent elements
                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                    // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                    int temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
        bubbleSort(arr);
        System.out.println("排序后的数组:");
        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}
```)
