public class BubbleSortExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                    // 交换arr[j]和arr[j+1]
                    int temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
        for (int num : arr) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}
```
### Correctness
- The code correctly implements the basic logic of the bubble sort algorithm. It repeatedly steps through the list, compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. After the sorting process, it prints the sorted array, which is in the expected ascending - order. There are no obvious logic errors in the sorting and printing parts.

### Readability
- The code is fairly easy to understand as the basic structure of the bubble sort algorithm is clearly presented. However, adding some comments to explain the outer and inner loops, especially the purpose of the `n - i - 1` condition in the inner loop, would make it even more accessible to new developers. Also, a method to encapsulate the sorting logic (instead of having it all in the `main` method) would improve modularity and readability.

### Efficiency
- The code has a time complexity of $O(n^2)$ in the worst and average cases, which is the typical time complexity for a basic bubble sort implementation. There are no obvious in - code optimizations done to reduce the number of comparisons or swaps. For example, adding a flag to break the outer loop early if no swaps are made in an iteration can improve the best - case time complexity to $O(n)$.

### Edge Cases
- The code does not handle edge cases such as an empty array or an array with only one element. While the current implementation will not throw an exception for these cases (it will just perform a minimal number of operations), it would be better to add some validation at the beginning of the sorting process to handle these cases gracefully.

### Best Practices
- As mentioned earlier, encapsulating the sorting logic in a separate method is a good practice. Also, following Java naming conventions more strictly (e.g., using more descriptive method and variable names) would be beneficial. For example, the array variable `arr` could be named `arrayToSort`. Additionally, using Java's built - in sorting methods (e.g., `Arrays.sort()`) is more efficient and reliable for most real - world scenarios where a simple sorting algorithm like bubble sort is not necessary. 
```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class BubbleSortExample {
    /**
     * 执行冒泡排序的方法。
     *
     * @param arrayToSort 要排序的整数数组
     * @return 排序后的整数数组
     */
    public static int[] bubbleSort(int[] arrayToSort) {
        if (arrayToSort == null || arrayToSort.length <= 1) {
            // 处理空数组或只有一个元素的数组的情况
            return arrayToSort;
        }
        int n = arrayToSort.length;
        boolean swapped;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            // 外层循环控制排序轮数，最多进行n - 1轮
            swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                // 内层循环在每一轮中比较相邻元素并进行交换
                // n - i - 1 是因为每一轮结束后，最大的元素已经“冒泡”到了数组末尾，
                // 所以下一轮不需要再比较已经排好序的元素
                if (arrayToSort[j] > arrayToSort[j + 1]) {
                    int temp = arrayToSort[j];
                    arrayToSort[j] = arrayToSort[j + 1];
                    arrayToSort[j + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            if (!swapped) {
                // 如果在一轮中没有发生交换，说明数组已经有序，可以提前结束排序
                break;
            }
        }
        return arrayToSort;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arrayToSort = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
        int[] sortedArray = bubbleSort(arrayToSort);
        for (int num : sortedArray) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }
}
```)
